-- Q1: hard / L4 — Consequential damages foreseeability (Hadley v. Baxendale)
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Baxter Manufacturing contracts with Summit Logistics to transport $45,000 of perishable goods to a retail buyer. The contract is silent about downstream sales. Summit delays delivery by four days, causing the goods to spoil. Baxter sues for $45,000 in lost goods plus $120,000 in lost profits from the retail resale. How should a court evaluate Baxter''s claim for the $120,000 in lost profits?', '["Lost profits are automatically recoverable as direct damages in any breach", "Lost profits are never recoverable because they are speculative", "Lost profits are recoverable only if Summit knew or had reason to know of the resale at the time of contracting", "Lost profits are recoverable regardless of foreseeability if Baxter can prove their exact amount"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under the rule from Hadley v. Baxendale, consequential damages such as lost profits are recoverable only if they were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party at the time of contract formation. Because the contract was silent about the downstream retail sale, Baxter must prove Summit knew or had reason to know of the resale arrangement. Unlike direct damages (the spoiled goods), consequential damages require this additional foreseeability showing and are not automatically awarded simply because the loss occurred.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Pre-existing duty rule
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Thornton hires Rivera, a licensed electrician, to rewire Thornton''s office for $8,000. Midway through the project, Rivera demands an additional $2,000, claiming the work is more difficult than expected. Thornton agrees to the extra payment to avoid delay. After Rivera completes the job, Thornton refuses to pay the additional amount. Under common law, is Thornton''s refusal enforceable?', '["No, because Thornton voluntarily agreed to the modification", "Yes, because Rivera provided no new consideration for the additional payment", "No, because increased difficulty constitutes new consideration", "Yes, but only if the original contract contained a no-modification clause"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under the common law pre-existing duty rule, a promise to pay more for work a party is already contractually obligated to perform is unenforceable for lack of new consideration. Rivera was already bound to complete the rewiring for $8,000 and offered nothing additional in exchange for the extra $2,000. Unlike UCC Article 2 (which permits good-faith modifications without new consideration under Section 2-209), common law requires a bargained-for exchange to support any contract modification.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Risk of loss FOB shipping point
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Chandler Electronics ships 200 tablets to Drake Retail under a contract specifying ''FOB shipping point.'' The carrier''s truck is involved in an accident during transit, destroying all 200 tablets. Drake has not yet received or inspected the goods. Which party bears the risk of loss for the destroyed tablets?', '["Drake, because risk passed when the goods were delivered to the carrier", "Chandler, because the goods never reached the destination", "The carrier, because risk transfers only upon delivery to the buyer", "Drake, but only if Drake had already paid for the goods"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under UCC Section 2-509, in a shipment contract (FOB shipping point), risk of loss passes to the buyer when the seller delivers the goods to the carrier. Because the contract specified FOB shipping point, Chandler''s obligation was fulfilled upon delivering the tablets to the carrier. Drake bears the financial loss even though the goods were destroyed in transit. Under a destination contract (FOB destination), risk would remain with the seller until tender at the destination.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Unconscionability (procedural + substantive)
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Garrison, an elderly non-English-speaking homeowner, signs a home repair contract with ProFix Corp. after a door-to-door salesperson presents the 30-page contract only in English and pressures Garrison to sign immediately. The contract requires Garrison to pay $50,000 for $3,000 worth of repairs, with a mandatory arbitration provision that prohibits any appeals. Evaluate the enforceability of this contract.', '["Enforceable, because Garrison voluntarily signed the contract", "Voidable only for procedural unconscionability due to the high-pressure tactics", "Voidable only for substantive unconscionability due to the $50,000 price term", "Likely unenforceable because both procedural and substantive unconscionability are present"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under the doctrine of unconscionability, courts examine both procedural elements (unfair bargaining process) and substantive elements (unreasonably one-sided terms). Procedural unconscionability exists here because of the language barrier, high-pressure tactics, and lack of meaningful choice. Substantive unconscionability exists because of the grossly inflated price ($50,000 for $3,000 of work) and the restrictive arbitration clause. Most courts require a showing of both types. A contract is not automatically enforceable merely because a party signed it.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Liquidated damages enforceability
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Fulton Construction contracts to build a commercial warehouse for Greer Properties by December 1. The contract includes a $1,500 per-day liquidated damages clause for late completion. Fulton finishes 20 days late, and Greer demands $30,000. Fulton argues the clause is an unenforceable penalty. Under what conditions is the liquidated damages clause enforceable?', '["Only if actual damages exceed the liquidated amount", "If the amount was a reasonable estimate of anticipated harm and actual damages were difficult to calculate at the time of contracting", "If the breaching party agreed to the clause, regardless of reasonableness", "Only if the non-breaching party proves it suffered exactly $30,000 in harm"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 356, a liquidated damages clause is enforceable when two conditions are met: (1) the amount is a reasonable forecast of the harm anticipated at the time of contracting, and (2) actual damages are difficult to estimate precisely. A $1,500 daily rate for a commercial warehouse project is generally reasonable given lost rental income and carrying costs. A clause that imposes a disproportionately large amount unrelated to anticipated harm is an unenforceable penalty, but reasonableness is judged at formation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — UCC modification vs. common law modification
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Newton Fabrication agrees to manufacture 1,000 steel brackets for Ogden Industries at $15 each. After a sudden steel shortage doubles raw material costs, Newton requests a price increase to $20 per bracket. Ogden agrees orally. Separately, Archer, an architect, contracts to design a building for Bishop for $50,000. Bishop later agrees orally to pay Archer an additional $10,000 for the same design work. Evaluate the enforceability of each modification.', '["Newton''s modification is enforceable under UCC Section 2-209 if made in good faith; Archer''s modification fails under common law for lack of new consideration", "Both modifications are enforceable because both parties voluntarily agreed", "Neither modification is enforceable because both lack new consideration", "Newton''s modification fails because it is oral; Archer''s is enforceable because services are not governed by the UCC"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under UCC Section 2-209(1), a contract for the sale of goods may be modified without new consideration, provided the modification is made in good faith. Newton''s steel shortage provides a legitimate commercial reason. Under common law, contract modifications require new consideration. Archer''s promise to perform the same design work for more money violates the pre-existing duty rule. The key distinction is that the UCC relaxes the consideration requirement for goods transactions while common law retains it for services.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Bilateral vs. unilateral contract
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'What distinguishes a unilateral contract from a bilateral contract?', '["A unilateral contract requires written form while a bilateral contract may be oral", "A unilateral contract involves two promises while a bilateral contract involves one", "A unilateral contract is accepted by performance while a bilateral contract is accepted by a return promise", "A unilateral contract is enforceable only between merchants"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under common law contract principles, a bilateral contract involves an exchange of mutual promises where both parties are bound upon the exchange. A unilateral contract involves a promise by the offeror that can be accepted only by the offeree''s performance, not by a return promise. For example, a reward offer (''I will pay $500 to anyone who finds my dog'') is unilateral because acceptance occurs through the act of finding the dog, not by promising to look.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Illusory promise
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'What is an illusory promise in contract law?', '["A promise made under duress that can be voided", "A promise to perform an illegal act", "A promise made without the other party''s knowledge", "A statement that appears to be a promise but does not actually commit the promisor to anything"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under common law consideration doctrine, an illusory promise is a statement that has the form of a promise but imposes no actual obligation on the promisor, such as ''I will buy goods from you if I feel like it.'' Because the promisor retains complete discretion, there is no bargained-for exchange and the promise cannot serve as consideration. Unlike a voidable promise made under duress, an illusory promise fails at formation because no binding commitment exists.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Duress defense (economic duress)
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Patterson, a small vendor, has a contract to supply parts to Weston Corp. for $50,000. Weston threatens to spread false information to Patterson''s other clients unless Patterson agrees to reduce the price to $30,000. Patterson signs an amendment reducing the price. After delivery, Patterson sues to recover the original $50,000. Which defense best supports Patterson''s claim?', '["Economic duress, because Weston''s wrongful threat left Patterson with no reasonable alternative", "Fraud, because Weston made a false statement about the value of the parts", "Undue influence, because Weston exercised a fiduciary relationship over Patterson", "Mutual mistake, because both parties misunderstood the fair market value"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under the doctrine of economic duress, a contract or modification is voidable when one party uses wrongful threats that leave the other party with no reasonable alternative but to agree. Weston''s threat to spread false information is an improper threat that goes beyond legitimate business pressure. Unlike fraud (which requires a material misrepresentation of fact), duress involves coercion that overcomes a party''s free will. The key element is the absence of a reasonable alternative to accepting the unfavorable terms.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Novation (substitution of parties)
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Franklin contracts to deliver consulting services to Morgan for $25,000. Franklin later wants to exit the contract, and all three parties — Franklin, Morgan, and a new consultant named Riley — agree that Riley will assume Franklin''s obligations and Franklin will be released from all liability. What type of contract arrangement has occurred?', '["Assignment, because Franklin transferred contractual rights to Riley", "Delegation, because Franklin transferred duties to Riley", "Novation, because all parties agreed to substitute Riley for Franklin and release Franklin", "Accord and satisfaction, because the original obligation was discharged by substitution"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A novation occurs when all original parties agree to substitute a new party for an existing one, completely releasing the original party from liability. The three essential elements are: (1) a valid existing obligation, (2) agreement by all parties, and (3) discharge of the original party. Unlike a delegation (where Franklin would remain secondarily liable), a novation requires the obligee''s (Morgan''s) consent to release the original obligor. An assignment transfers rights, not duties.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: easy / L2 — Counteroffer terminates original offer
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Hoffman offers to sell a vintage car to Klein for $18,000. Klein responds, ''I''ll pay $16,000.'' Hoffman rejects Klein''s counteroffer. Klein then says, ''I accept your original offer of $18,000.'' Is there a binding contract?', '["Yes, because Klein accepted the original terms within a reasonable time", "No, because Klein''s counteroffer terminated Hoffman''s original offer", "Yes, because Hoffman never formally revoked the original offer", "No, but only if Hoffman communicated a revocation before Klein''s acceptance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under common law, a counteroffer operates as both a rejection of the original offer and a new offer. When Klein proposed $16,000, Hoffman''s original $18,000 offer was extinguished. Klein''s subsequent attempt to accept the original $18,000 is actually a new offer that Hoffman is free to accept or reject. Unlike a mere inquiry (''Would you consider $16,000?''), Klein''s definitive statement constituted a counteroffer that terminated the power of acceptance.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Seller's remedies: resale + incidental damages
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Merritt Manufacturing contracts to sell 300 custom cabinets to Sawyer Design for $90,000 ($300 each). Before delivery, Sawyer repudiates the contract. Merritt resells the cabinets to another buyer for $75,000 and incurs $2,500 in additional storage and resale expenses. What is Merritt''s total recoverable damages?', '["$15,000 (contract price minus resale price)", "$90,000 (full contract price)", "$12,500 (resale shortfall minus expenses)", "$17,500 (contract price minus resale price plus incidental damages)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under UCC Section 2-706, when a buyer repudiates, the seller may resell the goods and recover the difference between the contract price and the resale price, plus incidental damages. Here: $90,000 - $75,000 = $15,000 shortfall, plus $2,500 in incidental damages (storage and resale costs) = $17,500 total. Unlike the full contract price remedy (Section 2-709, available only when goods cannot be resold), the resale remedy requires a commercially reasonable resale and includes incidental expenses.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Frustration of purpose vs. impossibility
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Lawton rents a storefront from Prescott for $5,000 per month specifically to operate a fireworks retail shop, as stated in the lease. The city subsequently passes an ordinance banning all fireworks sales within city limits. Separately, Garner contracts with Holt to renovate Holt''s beachfront cabin, but a hurricane completely destroys the cabin before work begins. Analyze how each party''s ability to avoid contractual obligations differs.', '["Both contracts are discharged under impossibility because neither party can perform", "Neither contract is discharged because both parties assumed the risk of changed circumstances", "Lawton''s contract may be discharged under frustration of purpose while Garner''s contract is discharged under impossibility", "Lawton''s contract is discharged under impossibility while Garner''s is discharged under frustration of purpose"]'::jsonb, 2, 'These scenarios illustrate two distinct discharge doctrines. Frustration of purpose applies to Lawton: the storefront still exists and the lease can technically be performed, but the principal purpose (selling fireworks) has been destroyed by the ordinance. Impossibility applies to Garner: the subject matter (the cabin) has been physically destroyed, making performance literally impossible. The key distinction is that frustration of purpose eliminates the value of performance while impossibility eliminates the ability to perform.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Option contract
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'What is the primary effect of an option contract?', '["It makes an offer irrevocable for a specified period in exchange for consideration", "It allows the offeree to change the terms of the offer", "It transfers the offeror''s rights to a third party", "It creates a binding obligation on both parties to perform immediately"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An option contract is a separate agreement in which the offeree pays consideration to keep the offeror''s offer open for a specified period. During that period, the offeror cannot revoke the offer. Unlike an ordinary offer (which can be revoked at any time before acceptance), an option contract binds the offeror to keep the offer available. Under UCC Section 2-205, a merchant''s firm offer achieves a similar result for up to three months without separate consideration.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Accord and satisfaction
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Benton owes Crawford $10,000 under a contract, but Benton disputes that the full amount is owed due to alleged defects in Crawford''s performance. Benton sends Crawford a check for $7,000 marked ''payment in full for all claims.'' Crawford cashes the check. Can Crawford later sue Benton for the remaining $3,000?', '["Yes, because cashing a check cannot waive legal rights", "No, because cashing the check constituted an accord and satisfaction that discharged the full debt", "Yes, because partial payment can never satisfy a larger undisputed debt", "No, but only if Benton can prove the defects reduced the contract value by exactly $3,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under the doctrine of accord and satisfaction, when there is a genuine dispute about the amount owed and one party offers a lesser amount clearly marked as ''payment in full,'' the other party''s acceptance (cashing the check) discharges the entire obligation. The three requirements are: (1) a bona fide dispute, (2) an offer of a specific lesser amount as full settlement, and (3) acceptance by the creditor. Unlike partial payment of an undisputed debt (which lacks consideration), a disputed claim provides the necessary consideration.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Revocation of acceptance (UCC 2-608)
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Norton purchases an industrial printing press from Quincy Equipment for $85,000. Norton accepts the press based on Quincy''s assurance that a persistent paper-jam issue would be repaired within two weeks. After six weeks and three failed repair attempts, the paper-jam issue persists, substantially impairing the press''s value. Under UCC Article 2, can Norton revoke acceptance?', '["No, because acceptance of goods is always final under the UCC", "No, because Norton should have rejected the goods upon initial delivery", "Yes, but only if Norton returns the press within 30 days of acceptance", "Yes, because Norton accepted on the reasonable assumption the nonconformity would be cured and it was not"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under UCC Section 2-608, a buyer may revoke acceptance when a nonconformity substantially impairs the value of the goods and the buyer accepted either (1) on the reasonable assumption the seller would cure, or (2) without discovering the nonconformity due to difficulty of discovery. Norton accepted based on Quincy''s cure promise, which went unfulfilled after six weeks. Unlike rejection (which must occur before acceptance), revocation of acceptance requires showing substantial impairment — a higher standard than the perfect tender rule.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Quasi-contract / unjust enrichment
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'What is a quasi-contract?', '["A court-imposed obligation to prevent unjust enrichment when no actual contract exists", "A contract formed by the actions of the parties rather than by written agreement", "A contract that is voidable at the option of one party", "A contract that requires court approval before it becomes binding"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A quasi-contract (also called a contract implied in law) is not a true contract but a legal fiction created by courts to prevent unjust enrichment. When one party confers a benefit on another and it would be unjust for the recipient to retain that benefit without payment, courts impose an obligation to pay the reasonable value of the benefit. Unlike an implied-in-fact contract (formed by the parties'' conduct), a quasi-contract is imposed by law regardless of the parties'' intent.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Adequacy of consideration
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Under common law, how do courts generally treat the adequacy of consideration?', '["Courts require consideration to be equal in value to what each party receives", "Courts require consideration to reflect fair market value", "Courts generally do not evaluate the adequacy of consideration if a bargained-for exchange exists", "Courts require an independent appraisal of each party''s consideration"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under common law, courts generally follow the principle that they will not inquire into the adequacy of consideration. As long as there is a bargained-for exchange of legal value, courts do not assess whether the exchange is proportionate. A party may agree to sell a $100,000 property for $1 if that reflects a genuine bargain. The exception is when grossly inadequate consideration serves as evidence of fraud, duress, or unconscionability, but inadequacy alone does not invalidate a contract.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Risk of loss: merchant vs. non-merchant seller
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Palmer, a furniture store owner, sells a dining table to Chen for $3,500. Chen pays in full but says she will pick up the table next Saturday. On Thursday, a fire destroys the store and the table. Under the same facts, if Palmer were a private individual selling through an online listing, how would the risk of loss analysis change?', '["In both cases, risk would be on Chen because she had already paid", "As a merchant, Palmer bears the risk until Chen takes physical possession; as a non-merchant, risk would pass when Palmer tendered delivery", "As a merchant, risk passes upon payment; as a non-merchant, risk passes upon physical possession", "In both cases, Palmer bears the risk because the goods were never delivered"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under UCC Section 2-509(3), when no carrier is involved, the distinction between merchant and non-merchant sellers is critical. A merchant seller retains risk of loss until the buyer takes physical possession of the goods. A non-merchant seller''s risk passes when the seller tenders delivery (makes goods available for pickup). Since Palmer is a merchant (furniture store owner), Palmer bears the loss. If Palmer were a private seller, risk would have passed upon tender, making Chen responsible. Payment alone does not determine risk allocation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Conditions precedent
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Delaney contracts to purchase Bennett''s house for $350,000, contingent on Delaney obtaining mortgage approval within 45 days. On day 30, Delaney is denied a mortgage but does not notify Bennett. On day 46, Bennett demands Delaney close the sale. Is Delaney obligated to perform?', '["Yes, because Delaney failed to notify Bennett of the denial within the 45-day period", "Yes, because the mortgage contingency only delays performance but does not excuse it", "No, but only if Delaney can prove the denial was not due to Delaney''s own fault", "No, because the condition precedent of mortgage approval was not satisfied and Delaney''s duty to perform never arose"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A condition precedent is an event that must occur before a party''s duty to perform is triggered. The mortgage approval clause is a classic condition precedent — Delaney''s obligation to purchase is contingent on obtaining financing. Because the mortgage was denied, the condition was not satisfied and Delaney''s duty to close never arose. Unlike a covenant (which creates an obligation to perform), a condition precedent suspends or eliminates the duty entirely. Failure to notify may create separate liability but does not transform the failed condition into a binding obligation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Puffery vs. express warranty
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Hanson, a car dealer, tells a customer, ''This is the finest sedan on the market today.'' During the same conversation, Hanson states, ''This sedan gets 35 miles per gallon on the highway.'' The customer purchases the car, which actually gets only 22 miles per gallon. Under UCC Section 2-313, which statement creates an express warranty?', '["Only the mileage statement, because it is a specific factual assertion", "Both statements, because any seller statement about goods creates a warranty", "Neither statement, because the seller did not use the word ''warranty''", "Only the ''finest sedan'' statement, because it relates to the quality of the car"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under UCC Section 2-313, an express warranty is created by any affirmation of fact or promise that relates to the goods and becomes part of the basis of the bargain. The mileage claim (35 miles per gallon) is a specific, verifiable factual assertion that constitutes an express warranty. The ''finest sedan'' statement is puffery — a general opinion or commendation that no reasonable buyer would rely on as fact. The seller need not use the word ''warranty'' for one to arise; the distinction turns on whether the statement is factual or merely opinion.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Incidental damages definition
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'In a breach of contract action, what are incidental damages?', '["Pre-agreed damages specified in the contract for breach", "Reasonable expenses incurred as a result of the breach, such as inspection, transportation, and storage costs", "Damages designed to punish the breaching party", "The difference between the contract price and the market price at the time of breach"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under UCC Sections 2-710 (seller) and 2-715(1) (buyer), incidental damages are the reasonable expenses directly resulting from the breach, including costs of inspection, transportation, storage, and arranging substitute performance. Unlike consequential damages (which compensate for additional foreseeable losses like lost profits), incidental damages cover the immediate out-of-pocket costs of dealing with the breach. They are distinct from liquidated damages (pre-agreed amounts) and punitive damages (generally not available in contract actions).', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Output and requirements contracts (UCC 2-306)
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Midwest Grain agrees to sell ''all the wheat produced on its 500-acre farm'' to Valley Mills for $6 per bushel. In a bumper crop year, Midwest produces 40% more wheat than its historical average and demands Valley Mills purchase the entire output. Under UCC Article 2, how is this dispute likely resolved?', '["Midwest may demand Valley Mills purchase all output regardless of quantity fluctuations", "The contract is unenforceable as illusory because the quantity term is not definite", "Valley Mills must accept the output but is not required to accept any quantity unreasonably disproportionate to the normal or estimated output", "Valley Mills may reject the entire shipment because the contract lacked a specific quantity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under UCC Section 2-306(1), an output contract (seller agrees to sell all it produces) is enforceable even without a stated quantity. However, the quantity tendered must not be unreasonably disproportionate to any stated estimate or, if no estimate exists, to any normal or otherwise comparable prior output. A 40% increase may be challenged as unreasonably disproportionate. Unlike illusory promises, output and requirements contracts impose a good-faith obligation that supplies sufficient consideration.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Illegality renders contract void
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Under common law, what is the effect of illegality on a contract?', '["The contract is voidable at the option of either party", "The contract is enforceable if both parties consented to the illegal purpose", "The contract is valid but subject to criminal penalties", "The contract is void and unenforceable from its inception"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under common law, a contract formed for an illegal purpose is void ab initio — it is treated as though it never existed and is completely unenforceable by either party. Courts will generally leave both parties where they stand, refusing to assist in enforcing illegal arrangements. Unlike a voidable contract (which remains valid until one party elects to avoid it), a void contract has no legal effect whatsoever. Neither party''s consent can cure the defect of illegality.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Restitution damages calculation
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Walters pays Henderson $15,000 as a deposit for a custom boat. Henderson begins construction, spending $8,000 on materials, but then breaches the contract by refusing to complete the boat. Walters sues for restitution. What amount is Walters most likely to recover?', '["$15,000, the deposit paid, to prevent Henderson''s unjust enrichment", "$8,000, the amount Henderson spent on materials", "$7,000, the deposit minus Henderson''s expenditures", "$23,000, the deposit plus Henderson''s material costs"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Restitution damages aim to restore the non-breaching party by requiring return of any benefit conferred on the breaching party to prevent unjust enrichment. Walters conferred $15,000 on Henderson, and because Henderson breached, Walters is entitled to recover the full deposit. Henderson''s own expenditures ($8,000 on materials) do not reduce the restitution amount — the breaching party cannot offset its own costs against the benefit received. Unlike expectation damages (which measure the benefit of the bargain), restitution measures the benefit actually conferred.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Undue influence definition
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'What constitutes undue influence as a defense to contract enforcement?', '["A material misrepresentation of fact made with intent to deceive", "Abuse of a position of trust or authority to pressure a party into an unfavorable contract", "Physical threats or coercion that overcome a party''s free will", "A genuine factual error shared by both parties at the time of contracting"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Undue influence occurs when a party in a position of trust, confidence, or authority over another exploits that relationship to induce the weaker party to enter into an unfavorable agreement. Common relationships include attorney-client, guardian-ward, and caretaker-dependent. Unlike duress (which involves threats or coercion), undue influence involves the subtle exploitation of an existing fiduciary or confidential relationship. It is also distinct from fraud, which requires a knowing misrepresentation of material fact.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Reliance damages
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Pearson promises to lease warehouse space to Grant for a two-year term beginning April 1. In reliance on the lease, Grant spends $12,000 on shelving, $5,000 on a security system, and $3,000 on business cards listing the warehouse address. Pearson refuses to honor the lease. Grant sues for reliance damages. What is Grant most likely to recover?', '["Only the $12,000 for shelving because it is directly related to the warehouse", "Nothing, because reliance damages are not available for lease contracts", "$20,000, representing all reasonable expenditures made in reliance on the lease", "$17,000, excluding the business cards because they are not substantial expenses"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Reliance damages reimburse the non-breaching party for expenditures reasonably incurred in reliance on the contract. All three expenses — shelving ($12,000), security system ($5,000), and business cards ($3,000) — were incurred in reasonable reliance on Pearson''s promise. Courts do not impose a minimum threshold for individual expenses; the test is whether the expenditure was reasonable and made in reliance on the contract. Unlike expectation damages (which project forward to the benefit of the bargain), reliance damages look backward to restore the party''s pre-contract position.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: hard / L3 — Impossibility of performance (subject matter destruction)
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Chambers, a renowned muralist, contracts to paint a custom mural on the exterior wall of Dixon''s building for $40,000. Before Chambers begins, a severe earthquake destroys the building wall entirely. Chambers offers to paint the mural on a different wall, but Dixon refuses and demands Chambers find a way to reconstruct the original wall and complete the mural. Under common law, what is the likely outcome?', '["Chambers must reconstruct the wall and complete the mural because the contract did not include a force majeure clause", "Dixon may obtain specific performance ordering Chambers to complete the mural on any available wall", "Chambers is liable for breach because a personal services contract cannot be discharged by impossibility", "The contract is discharged by impossibility because the specific subject matter has been destroyed through no fault of either party"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under the doctrine of impossibility, a contract is discharged when performance becomes objectively impossible due to the destruction of the subject matter essential to performance, provided neither party is at fault. The specific building wall was the subject matter of the contract, and its destruction makes performance as agreed impossible. The absence of a force majeure clause does not prevent application of the common law impossibility doctrine — it exists as a default rule. Unlike mere difficulty or increased cost, physical destruction of the subject matter clearly satisfies the impossibility standard.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Written promise to pay debt barred by statute of limitations
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Reynolds owed $8,000 to Canton under a contract. The statute of limitations has expired, making the debt legally unenforceable. Reynolds writes and signs a letter to Canton stating, ''I acknowledge I owe you $8,000 and promise to pay it by June 30.'' Canton seeks to enforce this promise. Is the written promise enforceable?', '["Yes, because a written promise to pay a debt barred by the statute of limitations is enforceable without new consideration", "No, because the expired statute of limitations permanently extinguishes the debt", "Yes, but only if Canton provides new consideration for the promise", "No, because the original contract can never be revived once the limitations period expires"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under common law, a written promise to pay a debt barred by the statute of limitations is an exception to the consideration requirement. The debtor''s signed written acknowledgment effectively revives the obligation and restarts the limitations period. The rationale is that the debtor''s moral obligation plus the writing provides sufficient basis for enforcement without new bargained-for consideration. Unlike an oral acknowledgment (which is generally insufficient), the writing requirement provides evidence of the debtor''s deliberate intent to assume the obligation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Seller's comprehensive remedies analysis
(3, 'Business Law: Contracts', 'Atlas Manufacturing contracts to sell 2,000 specialized circuit boards to Beacon Electronics for $200,000 ($100 each). After Atlas completes manufacturing, Beacon repudiates the contract. Atlas resells the boards to another buyer for $120,000, incurring $4,000 in storage costs and $1,500 in resale brokerage fees. Evaluate Atlas''s available remedies and determine the optimal recovery.', '["Sue for cover damages of $80,000 under UCC Section 2-712", "Resell and recover $85,500 under UCC Section 2-706 (contract-resale differential plus incidental damages)", "Recover the full contract price of $200,000 under UCC Section 2-709 because the goods are specialized", "Recover only $80,000 under UCC Section 2-708 (contract-market differential) without incidental damages"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under UCC Section 2-706, when a seller resells goods after buyer repudiation, the seller may recover the difference between the contract price and the resale price, plus incidental damages. Here: $200,000 - $120,000 = $80,000 shortfall, plus $4,000 storage and $1,500 brokerage fees = $85,500 total. The full contract price remedy (Section 2-709) is unavailable because Atlas was able to resell. Cover damages (Section 2-712) are a buyer''s remedy, not a seller''s. The resale remedy is optimal because it includes incidental damages.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
